      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "While well-intentioned, the reliance on intensive management often masks a fundamental human desire for control over nature's inherent unpredictability. These interventions, like constant culling or specific planting schemes, are essentially high-maintenance patches that require perpetual human oversight to prevent ecological drift. This model fails in the long term because it never resolves the underlying pressures, instead kicking them down the road through continuous, resource-intensive inputs. Rewilding, conversely, bets on the ecosystem's resilience to achieve self-sustainability rather than perpetually staffing a nature project.",
      "source": "target",
