      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The most definitive argument is that traditional, managed conservation often treats symptoms rather than ecological causes, treating nature as a fragile artifact needing constant human maintenance. Scientifically, these interventions struggle with the inherent complexity and feedback loops of natural systems, often leading to unpredictable, localized successes rather than resilient, landscape-scale recovery. Rewilding offers a scientifically robust alternative by embracing ecological processes, allowing natural disturbances to guide adaptation and self-correction. Ultimately, controlled maintenance requires perpetual resources, whereas functioning ecosystems generate their own durability.",
      "source": "target",
